<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Material 3 fade, screen that is LEAVING (push: the screen underneath; pop:
  the one being dismissed).

  Same file name as a react-native-screens resource: an app resource replaces
  the library's one when Gradle merges resources, so react-native-screens
  (4.28.0, pinned in package.json) runs this without being patched. The stock
  file is a plain 150ms crossfade that runs in parallel with the incoming one.

  105ms on the emphasized curve: fast (the curve is front-loaded, so it is
  ~90% transparent before the halfway mark of the real time). That speed is the
  point — the arriving screen now starts *with* it rather than after it (see
  rns_fade_in.xml), so this half has to get out of the way quickly or the two
  would stack as two equally opaque screens. It stays well inside the same
  300ms Material budget, and on a pop it is what makes the dismissed screen
  clear out fast enough for the revealed one to read as already there.

  The background behind both screens is the same #101214
  (`colors.background` == `colors.surface`), so the brief moment where neither
  screen is fully opaque shows no flash.

  Assigned to `animation: 'fade'` by src/navigation/RootNavigator.tsx.

  Re-check this file when react-native-screens is bumped: a qualifier-specific
  variant (anim-v33/…) or a rename would stop this override winning, silently.
-->
<alpha xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:fromAlpha="1"
    android:toAlpha="0"
    android:startOffset="0"
    android:duration="105"
    android:interpolator="@interpolator/pixi_emphasized" />
